  <section class="kata-section" aria-labelledby="kata-what">
    <h2 id="kata-what">What it does</h2>
    <ul class="kata-features">
      <li><strong>New in 2.0: four loops</strong>Load up to four loops and KATA slices them together, matched to your song's tempo and key, each with its own level and shift.</li>
      <li><strong>New in 2.0: effect buses</strong>Send any step to bus A or B. Each bus is a rack of filter, drive, crush, delay and reverb that you switch on or off and drag into any order.</li>
      <li><strong>New in 2.0: video effects</strong>Flash, invert, punch, shake, colour split, pixel and mono pulse with every hit, on screen, in the Float window and in video bounces.</li>
      <li><strong>New in 2.0: drag into Live</strong>Grab the WAV, MIDI or MP4 tile and drop it on a track: KATA makes the file as you drag.</li>
      <li><strong>Four ways to slice</strong>At the hits, in the quiet gaps, on a beat grid or into equal parts. Drag a chop line to move it; it snaps to the nearest hit.</li>
      <li><strong>Patterns that listen</strong>KATA hears how deep, bright and loud each slice is: low hits go on the downbeats, bright ones on the ticks, loud mid ones on the backbeats.</li>
      <li><strong>Styles to steer it</strong>Four-on-the-floor, broken beats, halftime, afro house, amapiano and club, or Original to keep the loop's own order.</li>
      <li><strong>Lock and shuffle</strong>Right-click a step to lock it. Shuffle rolls the rest. The History strip brings back any of the last eight patterns.</li>
      <li><strong>Feel</strong>Swing, Skip, Drift, Wobble and Tight change how the pattern breathes while it plays. Each step can repeat, change pitch, play backwards or fade.</li>
      <li><strong>Constellation</strong>Every slice is a star and the pattern is drawn as lines between them. Click a star to hear it; drag it onto a step, or onto another star to swap them.</li>
      <li><strong>Slice a video</strong>Drop an MP4 or MOV (up to two minutes, Mac only). The picture follows whatever slice plays. Float opens it in its own window, ready for a projector.</li>
      <li><strong>Bounce</strong>Render 1 to 16 bars as a WAV and, for a video, an MP4 with the picture re-cut to match.</li>
      <li><strong>Play it yourself</strong>Every slice is on a key from C1 up. In Hold mode the pattern runs only while you hold a key below C1.</li>
      <li><strong>Made for laptops</strong>Light and dark themes, and the window at 100, 125 or 150 %.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="kata-section" aria-labelledby="kata-install">
    <h2 id="kata-install">Install on a Mac</h2>
    <div>
      <ol class="kata-steps">
        <li>Unzip the download and copy <code>KATA.vst3</code> to <code>~/Library/Audio/Plug-Ins/VST3/</code> and <code>KATA.clap</code> to <code>~/Library/Audio/Plug-Ins/CLAP/</code> (in Finder: Go › Go to Folder…).</li>
        <li>KATA is free and unsigned, so macOS blocks it after downloading. Open Terminal and paste:<br><code>xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine ~/Library/Audio/Plug-Ins/VST3/KATA.vst3</code><br><code>xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine ~/Library/Audio/Plug-Ins/CLAP/KATA.clap</code></li>
        <li>Rescan plug-ins in your host (in Live: Settings › Plug-Ins › Rescan). KATA is an instrument: put it on a MIDI track.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="kata-note">The download includes a README with these steps, the manual and the full source code. Updating from 1.0: copy the new files over the old ones; sets made with 1.0 open in 2.0 and sound the same. On Windows, KATA 1.0 is in the same folder: copy the two files into your VST3 and CLAP folders (audio loops only).</p>
    </div>
  </section>

